# Margin

**Tagline:** Learn what you read. Private by default, cloud when it counts.

**Platform category:** Web (Chrome) — Chrome extension (Gemini Nano via Firebase AI Logic hybrid inference)

---

## Inspiration

Everyone who reads to learn — students, med and law candidates, engineers picking up a new stack, language learners — hits the same wall: the forgetting curve. Spaced repetition (Anki and friends) is the best-evidenced fix we have, but making good cards by hand is slow, so most people never start or quietly stop.

AI card generators exist, but they all ship your reading to a server. That's a non-starter for the things people most need to remember: lecture notes, internal docs, patient case write-ups, contracts, a personal journal in a new language. And they stop working on the train.

Firebase AI Logic's hybrid inference made a different design possible: **do the private, everyday work on-device with Gemini Nano, and only reach for cloud Gemini when the input genuinely needs it — and tell the user every time.**

## What it does

Margin is a Chrome extension that turns anything you read into spaced-repetition flashcards:

- **Any source:** selected text (right-click), the whole page (readability extraction), a PDF open in the tab, a YouTube video's transcript, a screenshot or image, or pasted notes.
- **Hybrid router that explains itself:** every card carries a route badge — *On-device · Gemini Nano* or *Cloud · Gemini* — plus the reason ("short text fits the on-device budget", "PDF input runs in the cloud", "Nano unavailable on this device", "kept on-device: sensitive content detected", "privacy mode").
- **On-device sensitivity gate:** before any cloud call, a regex PII scan plus a Gemini Nano structured-output classification checks the content. If it's sensitive, the job is pinned on-device (chunked to fit), or the user is asked.
- **Privacy mode:** one toggle switches the router to `ONLY_ON_DEVICE`; nothing leaves the machine.
- **Offline queue:** jobs that genuinely need the cloud while you're offline are queued and run automatically when you reconnect; on-device jobs keep working offline.
- **Review with on-device answer grading:** type your answer; Gemini Nano compares it to the card and suggests a rating (Again/Hard/Good/Easy) with what you missed. Your answers never leave the device.
- **Real spaced repetition:** FSRS scheduling (via the open-source ts-fsrs), due counts, keyboard shortcuts, next-interval previews on each button.
- **Edit, decks, export:** inline editing, decks, de-duplication, export to Anki (.apkg) and Anki-compatible TSV.
- **Source anchors:** each card links back to the exact passage via a text-fragment URL.
- **Stats:** share of cards generated on-device, and bytes that never left your machine.

## How we built it

- **Firebase AI Logic (firebase/ai, JS SDK 13.4.0)** — `getAI(app, { backend: new GoogleAIBackend() })` and `getGenerativeModel(ai, { mode, inCloudParams })` with all four `InferenceMode`s chosen per job by our router. We call `initializeDeviceModel()` from an explicit user gesture ("Enable on-device AI") with a download progress bar, use `responseSchema` structured JSON output for cards, grading, and sensitivity, and read `response.inferenceSource` to label every result truthfully.
- **Router:** a pure, unit-tested `decide(job, ctx)` function considers input type (text / PNG-JPEG image / PDF), estimated tokens vs. the on-device budget (9,216-token limit; default budget from Remote Config), Nano availability, online state, sensitivity, and privacy mode — and always returns a human-readable reason.
- **Firebase App Check** (required by AI Logic) with the debug provider for the extension origin; **Remote Config** supplies router thresholds and model names (`gemini-3.5-flash-lite` default cloud model, `gemini-3.8-flash` for long documents) with safe in-code defaults.
- **Chrome MV3:** side panel, popup, review page, options page, context menus, service worker for messaging only (all inference runs in extension pages where the Prompt API is exposed). Everything is bundled with esbuild — no remote code. pdf.js for local PDF text extraction, sql.js for building .apkg files.
- **Testing:** node:test unit tests for the router, parser, de-dupe, scheduler and exporters; Playwright end-to-end tests that load the unpacked extension in Chromium using a deterministic mock provider (Demo mode) so the whole flow is testable without a Firebase project or Nano.

## Challenges we ran into

- **Choosing the mode per request, not per app.** `PREFER_ON_DEVICE` alone hides important decisions (a PDF silently goes to the cloud). We built the router on top so users can see and control those decisions.
- **The on-device token budget.** Long pages exceed the 9,216-token on-device limit; we chunk with overlap, de-duplicate across chunks, and fall back to a long-context cloud model when content isn't sensitive.
- **Reliable JSON from a small model.** Structured output plus a tolerant parser (code fences, trailing commas, truncated arrays) and validation/caps.
- **Extension constraints:** MV3 CSP (no remote code, wasm for sql.js), the Prompt API not being available in service workers, and the user-activation requirement for model download.
- **App Check on a `chrome-extension://` origin**, where reCAPTCHA isn't an option — debug provider for the submission (accepted by the rules), documented path to a custom provider for production.

## Accomplishments that we're proud of

- A hybrid experience where the *split* between device and cloud is a feature users understand, not an implementation detail.
- Private, offline answer grading during review.
- A complete loop — capture → generate → edit → review → export — not a demo of one prompt.

## What we learned

- On-device models are great at short, structured, private tasks (classification, grading, short-passage card writing); the cloud wins on long documents and PDFs. Designing around that split makes both better.
- Good flashcards follow the minimum-information principle; prompting for atomic cards and cloze deletions matters more than model size.

## What's next

- Android companion (Gemini Nano 4 via Firebase AI Logic) for reviewing on the go, synced through Firestore with Firebase Authentication.
- Image occlusion cards from diagrams.
- Shared decks for study groups, with per-deck privacy settings.

## Built with

TypeScript, Firebase AI Logic (firebase/ai hybrid inference), Gemini Nano (Chrome Prompt API), Gemini API (gemini-3.5-flash-lite, gemini-3.8-flash), Firebase App Check, Firebase Remote Config, Chrome Extensions Manifest V3 (Side Panel, Context Menus), esbuild, ts-fsrs (FSRS), pdf.js, sql.js, IndexedDB, Playwright, Node.js test runner.
# Margin — demo video script (target 2:45, hard cap 3:00)

Recording setup: Chrome desktop (139+) with `chrome://flags/#prompt-api-for-gemini-nano-multimodal-input` enabled and the Gemini Nano model downloaded; Margin loaded unpacked with a real Firebase project config + registered App Check debug token. Screen at 1920x1080, browser zoom 110%. Record voice-over separately. Add English captions (YouTube auto-captions + manual fix).

| # | Time | Shot (what is on screen) | Voice-over |
|---|------|--------------------------|------------|
| 1 | 0:00–0:12 | Cold open: a long Wikipedia-style article scrolls; cut to Margin icon. | "You read something great today. In a week you'll remember about a fifth of it. Margin fixes that — privately." |
| 2 | 0:12–0:30 | Highlight a paragraph → right-click → "Make flashcards from selection". Side panel opens, cards stream in. Zoom on green **On-device · Gemini Nano** badge with reason "short text, fits on-device budget". | "Select anything, right-click, and Margin writes atomic, Anki-quality flashcards. This ran on Gemini Nano, on my laptop, through Firebase AI Logic. Nothing left the machine." |
| 3 | 0:30–0:50 | Turn Wi-Fi off (show OS toggle). Generate cards from another paragraph — still works, badge On-device. Then try "Whole page" on a 30-page doc: card shows "Queued — cloud needed (long document), will run when online". Wi-Fi on → queue drains, blue **Cloud · Gemini** badge, reason "long document, exceeds on-device budget". | "Offline? Short stuff still works on-device. Long documents get queued and hand off to cloud Gemini the moment I'm back online — same SDK, one line of config." |
| 4 | 0:50–1:10 | Paste notes containing an email address and a phone number. Badge: **Kept on-device — sensitive content detected**. Open the route details popover showing the decision trace. | "Before anything goes to the cloud, Nano checks it for personal data. If it finds some, the job is pinned on-device. Every card tells you where it was made and why." |
| 5 | 1:10–1:30 | PDF open in a tab → "From this PDF" (cloud, reason "PDF input not supported on-device"). YouTube lecture → "From this video transcript". Screenshot of a diagram → image cards from Nano (PNG on-device). | "PDFs, YouTube transcripts, screenshots — Margin picks the best model for each input, automatically." |
| 6 | 1:30–2:05 | Open Review. Card front → type an answer → Nano grades it: "Mostly right — you missed X" → suggested rating Good. Show FSRS next-interval labels on the 4 buttons (1m / 10m / 3d / 8d). Keyboard shortcuts. | "Reviewing is where memory is built. Type your answer and Gemini Nano grades it on-device — your answers stay private, and it works on a plane. Scheduling uses FSRS, the modern spaced-repetition algorithm." |
| 7 | 2:05–2:25 | Edit a card inline, move it to a deck, then Export → Anki package (.apkg) and TSV. Import into desktop Anki (optional cut). | "Edit, organise into decks, and export to Anki with one click — your cards are yours." |
| 8 | 2:25–2:40 | Options/Stats: "87% of cards generated on-device · 412 KB never sent to the cloud". Toggle Privacy mode (ONLY_ON_DEVICE). Remote Config values shown (thresholds/model names). | "A stats panel shows how much stayed local. Privacy mode forces on-device only. Thresholds and model names come from Firebase Remote Config, and App Check protects the cloud path." |
| 9 | 2:40–2:50 | Logo + tagline + repo URL. | "Margin. Learn what you read — private by default, cloud when it counts." |

B-roll / fallback: if live Nano is unavailable while recording, the extension's Demo mode simulates both routes — but say so on screen ("Demo mode") rather than presenting it as live inference.

