diff --git a/submission/devpost-story.md b/submission/devpost-story.md
index ab043db565ac5e9b6a0531ade5ff319203757fd1..4309927d6d0ca1c84b785f843ce5c9387d1d1e71
--- a/submission/devpost-story.md
+++ b/submission/devpost-story.md
@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@
 
 Everyone who reads to learn — students, med and law candidates, engineers picking up a new stack, language learners — hits the same wall: the forgetting curve. Spaced repetition (Anki and friends) is the best-evidenced fix we have, but making good cards by hand is slow, so most people never start or quietly stop.
 
-AI card generators exist, but they all ship your reading to a server. That's a non-starter for the things people most need to remember: lecture notes, internal docs, patient case write-ups, contracts, a personal journal in a new language. And they stop working on the train.
+Sending source material to a server can rule out AI card generation for private reading such as patient case write-ups, internal docs, or a personal journal. A medical student can use Margin to turn sensitive case notes into cards on-device, then practice recall offline.
 
 Firebase AI Logic's hybrid inference made a different design possible: **do the private, everyday work on-device with Gemini Nano, and only reach for cloud Gemini when the input genuinely needs it — and tell the user every time.**
 
@@ -18,15 +18,15 @@
 
 Margin is a Chrome extension that turns anything you read into spaced-repetition flashcards:
 
-- **Any source:** selected text (right-click), the whole page (readability extraction), a PDF open in the tab, a YouTube video's transcript, a screenshot or image, or pasted notes.
-- **Hybrid router that explains itself:** every card carries a route badge — *On-device · Gemini Nano* or *Cloud · Gemini* — plus the reason ("short text fits the on-device budget", "PDF input runs in the cloud", "Nano unavailable on this device", "kept on-device: sensitive content detected", "privacy mode").
-- **On-device sensitivity gate:** before any cloud call, a regex PII scan plus a Gemini Nano structured-output classification checks the content. If it's sensitive, the job is pinned on-device (chunked to fit), or the user is asked.
-- **Privacy mode:** one toggle switches the router to `ONLY_ON_DEVICE`; nothing leaves the machine.
-- **Offline queue:** jobs that genuinely need the cloud while you're offline are queued and run automatically when you reconnect; on-device jobs keep working offline.
+- **Capture sources** include selected text, whole pages via main-content extraction, PDFs, YouTube captions, screenshots, images, and pasted notes.
+- **Hybrid routing** shows an **On-device**, **Kept local**, or **Cloud** badge with a reason in the preview, Cards, and Review. Auto prefers Gemini Nano for short eligible captures and records when Firebase AI Logic falls back to cloud.
+- **On-device sensitivity checks** look for patterns in source text and use Gemini Nano classification on eligible cloud-capable text routes when Nano is available. Sensitive text stays local. A pattern-only match can ask for approval when Nano is unavailable.
+- **Privacy mode** requires `ONLY_ON_DEVICE`. No source content leaves your machine. A configured build still fetches Firebase Remote Config settings.
+- **Offline queue** stores captures that need cloud inference. Keep the side panel open for automatic retries while online. On-device generation remains available offline when Nano is ready.
 - **Review with on-device answer grading:** type your answer; Gemini Nano compares it to the card and suggests a rating (Again/Hard/Good/Easy) with what you missed. Your answers never leave the device.
 - **Real spaced repetition:** FSRS scheduling (via the open-source ts-fsrs), due counts, keyboard shortcuts, next-interval previews on each button.
-- **Edit, decks, export:** inline editing, decks, de-duplication, export to Anki (.apkg) and Anki-compatible TSV.
-- **Source anchors:** each card links back to the exact passage via a text-fragment URL.
+- **Cards** lets you search saved cards, edit them inline, and move them across decks. Margin deduplicates generated cards and exports Anki packages and TSV files.
+- **Source anchors** link selections and verifiable page excerpts via text fragments. Cards from page captures fall back to the page URL when an excerpt is unavailable.
 - **Stats:** share of cards generated on-device, and bytes that never left your machine.
 
 ## How we built it
@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@
 
 ## Challenges we ran into
 
-- **Choosing the mode per request, not per app.** `PREFER_ON_DEVICE` alone hides important decisions (a PDF silently goes to the cloud). We built the router on top so users can see and control those decisions.
+- **Choosing the mode per request.** Short eligible captures prefer Nano, sensitive content requires Nano, long documents prefer the cloud, and scanned PDFs and videos without accessible captions require cloud inference.
 - **The on-device token budget.** Long pages exceed the 9,216-token on-device limit; we chunk with overlap, de-duplicate across chunks, and fall back to a long-context cloud model when content isn't sensitive.
 - **Reliable JSON from a small model.** Structured output plus a tolerant parser (code fences, trailing commas, truncated arrays) and validation/caps.
 - **Extension constraints:** MV3 CSP (no remote code, wasm for sql.js), the Prompt API not being available in service workers, and the user-activation requirement for model download.
@@ -53,7 +53,7 @@
 
 ## What we learned
 
-- On-device models are great at short, structured, private tasks (classification, grading, short-passage card writing); the cloud wins on long documents and PDFs. Designing around that split makes both better.
+- Nano handles short structured tasks such as classification, grading, and card generation. Margin extracts readable PDF text locally. Scanned PDFs and long nonsensitive documents benefit from the cloud route.
 - Good flashcards follow the minimum-information principle; prompting for atomic cards and cloze deletions matters more than model size.
 
 ## What's next
